Project Aegis: Tactical HUD (Next.js Frontend) Reconstruction Manifest
This manifest serves as the core design and functional bible for rebuilding the Aegis glass. The objective is to transition from a generic developer dashboard to a Combat-Certified C4ISR Tactical Interface that adheres to MIL-SPEC standards while maintaining the high-velocity throughput of the Rust/Python backend.

1. Design Language: The MIL-SPEC Standard
The UI must feel like a mission-critical tool found in a Tactical Operations Center (TOC). It must prioritize data density and legibility over aesthetic "fluff."

Primary Design Bible: MIL-STD-2525D (Joint Military Symbology) and MIL-STD-1472H (Human Engineering).

Tactical Color Palette:

Background: Deep Slate/Off-Black (#121212) to reduce eye strain in low-light SOC environments.

Friendly/Nominal: Cyan/Tactical Blue (#00FFFF) or Neutral Green (#00FF00).

Hostile/Critical: True Red (#FF0000) for all detected Sigma/IOB threats.

Unknown/Pending: Tactical Yellow (#FFFF00).

Typography:

Forensic Data: Monospaced (e.g., Fira Code or JetBrains Mono) for perfect vertical alignment of PIDs, timestamps, and hashes.

Interface: Clean, high-legibility sans-serif (e.g., Inter or Roboto).

Symbology: Implement diamond frames for hostile process detections and circle frames for verified system processes.

2. Core Functional Modules
A. The Commander’s Overview (W5 Brief Pane)
Function: Real-time display of the Gemini-3.1-Flash-Lite synthesized intelligence.

Requirement: Must be the focal point of the HUD. When a Critical sequence completes, this pane auto-populates with the Who, What, When, Where, and Why narrative.

Visual: Styled as a formal "Spot Report" or "SITREP."

B. The High-Velocity Telemetry Feed (79k EPS Buffer)
Function: A rolling view of the raw forensic logs.

Requirement: Use a virtualized list (e.g., react-window or TanStack Virtual) to prevent DOM collapse.

Throttle: Implement a 100ms UI batching window. The backend may be hitting 79,000 EPS, but the UI should only update at a rate readable by the human eye (~10-20 alerts per second max).

C. Provenance Graph (Operation Ghost Hunter)
Function: A visual representation of the Process Lineage reconstructed by the Rust DashMap.

Requirement: D3.js or React Flow implementation to show the "Parent -> Child" execution chain.

Interactivity: Clicking a node must pull the SHA-256 hash and DLL list from the Operation Black Box forensic vault.

D. The Kill Chain Interface (SOAR Control)
Function: Actionable remediation buttons tied to the Python/PowerShell backend.

Requirement: Buttons for "ISOLATE HOST," "TERMINATE PID," and "SEAL REGISTRY HIVE."

Safety: Must include a two-stage "Safety Off" confirmation to prevent accidental disruption of critical system processes.

3. Technical Implementation & Bridge
Framework: Next.js 14/15 (App Router) for optimized server-side rendering and routing.

Real-Time Data: WebSockets (Socket.io) or FastMCP connection to the Python triage layer. Avoid REST polling to ensure sub-100ms "Left of Bang" latency.

State Management: Lightweight stores (Zustand) for tactical alert counts and the active forensic manifest.

Styling: Tailwind CSS for rapid layout of the 12-column tactical grid.

4. Mandatory Compliance Features (Federal/DoD)
Access Banner: The login screen must feature the standard DoD System Use Notification Banner for NIST 800-53 compliance.

Audit Trail Visualization: A dedicated tab for AU-6/AU-11 reports, showing the cryptographic chain of custody (SHA-256) for every forensic artifact stored in the vault.

Session Security: Automatic logout and "Clear Screen" functionality per DISA STIG requirements for classified/sensitive environments.